innovations.problem:
In the early 2000s, the two co-founders began with two key observations, which they still consider relevant 10 years on:
The quest for meaning, both on an individual and collective level, which permeates society as a whole;
Social division or fragmentation, which is becoming increasingly pronounced in society

innovations.description:
Volunteers and service users are invited to identify five essential personal skills required to take control of one’s own life and play an active role in society:
Persuading without manipulating: knowing how to present arguments, defend a point of view and listen to the person you are speaking to;
Finding information and avoiding misinformation: knowing how to use internet search engines, identify different sources of information, cross-check sources and verify their relevance;
Nurturing and enriching one’s social environment: building and strengthening one’s network, staying connected to avoid isolation;
Understanding how the modern world works: key issues and power dynamics, the economy, politics, globalisation and citizenship;
Developing self-confidence and self-awareness.
To pass on these skills, Les Cités d’Or has set up ‘L’école buissonnière’, a series of educational workshops.
Over a six-month period, each ‘L’école buissonnière’ session enables 14 young people on civic service placements to alternate between carrying out an assignment at a partner organisation and undertaking one of the following three types of collective project, coordinated by Les Cités d’Or:
A public hearing. The 14 participants choose a public figure (from the worlds of sport, the arts, politics, etc.) whose life story resonates with them and become ‘investigators of life and meaning’, to use the association’s own words. At the end of their investigation, they invite this public figure to speak and contribute to the discussion themselves through their questions.
The issue addressed must have a direct or indirect impact on the participant’s life;
The topic deals with an obstacle the participant has encountered in their life.
A participatory investigation. Working in pairs, the young people set out to explore the human and intangible heritage of their local area, bringing back highlights which they showcase in the format of their choice: a photo exhibition, an audio library or a giant treasure hunt.
Throughout the programme, the Cités d’Or team supports the implementation of these projects through group guidance.
Alongside this, a second type of support is provided by an adult volunteer, referred to as a ‘mentor’ by the charity. The scheme is open to anyone aged 16 or over: young people who are in education or have left school, and those living in the city centre or the suburbs.

