innovations.description:
As part of an “outreach” approach based on collaboration and partnership with various local stakeholders (social housing providers, cultural and sociocultural institutions, schools, nonprofit organizations, citizen councils, residents, and public authorities), PaQ’la Lune carries out “art projects” in about a dozen priority neighborhoods (QPVs), particularly on the streets or at the base of apartment buildings. There, it offers various activities for exploring and practicing the arts, as well as outreach tools that contribute to the realization of cultural rights, with a particular focus on youth.
PaQ’la Lune’s activities are divided into three areas: cultural and social events, artistic practice activities, and artistic creations. The program is developed collectively.
Cultural and social events:
street and building-front events;
discussion cafés, social gatherings, picnics;
street libraries and traveling reading corners;
exploratory outings, particularly to the city’s cultural sites, to venture beyond the neighborhood.
Artistic practice activities:
after-school workshops in partnership with municipalities;
art and cultural education workshops and programs during school hours in partnership with the National Ministry of Education, the DRAC, and local authorities;
workshops, courses, and “cultural Wednesdays” during free time in partnership with community centers.
Artistic projects:
artist residencies organised at the heart of neighbourhoods in partnership with local councils, social housing providers, community centres and libraries, featuring public rehearsals and an artistic presence in the neighbourhood’s daily life through impromptu performances;
performances held in schools, libraries, building lobbies or even a neighbour’s flat.

