innovations.evidence:
The programme has grown from a Hungarian educational initiative into a large-scale professional and institutional network with national reach and international implementation. Its current results include:
more than 150,000 children and young people reached;
more than 9,000 educators involved;
more than 1,800 participating educational institutions;
implementation in eight countries;
age-adapted materials for four educational stages, complemented by resources for learners with special educational needs and learning difficulties;
more than 63,000 educator implementation reports uploaded to the programme platform.
The programme’s effectiveness has been examined through two large-sample, longitudinal, controlled studies conducted in 2016 and 2025 by the Positive Psychology Research Group of Eötvös Loránd University, involving almost 1,000 students. The studies compared participating students with control groups before and after the school-year programme.
The findings identified significant positive changes in participants’ emotional, cognitive and psychological functioning. Students taking part in the programme demonstrated stronger positive emotional orientation, optimism, confidence in the future, self-compassion, self-confidence, self-efficacy, flexible adaptation, problem-solving and creative thinking. The results also indicated reduced anxiety. Compared with their control-group peers, participating students were more focused, persistent, creative and socially open, while their coping capacity, resilience and psychological immunity were also strengthened.
Qualitative feedback from educators complements these research findings. Participating teachers report improvements in classroom atmosphere, cooperation, conflict management, problem-solving, perseverance and students’ ability to recover from difficulties. Further research and implementation experience have also explored the programme’s relevance for learners with learning difficulties, the development of a positive classroom climate, social sensitivity and the prevention of peer victimisation.
At institutional level, the programme:
provides educators with a practical and immediately applicable mental health promotion toolkit;
strengthens professional confidence and reflective pedagogical practice;
supports more open communication about emotions, needs and difficulties;
encourages inclusive, supportive and cooperative classroom relationships;
creates a professional community in which educators exchange experiences and learn from one another;
helps institutions move from isolated well-being activities towards a coherent, preventive and sustainable whole-school approach.
The programme has also received significant external recognition. It received the Silver Award of the OTP Fáy Foundation Educational Innovation Award in 2024, was selected for the HundrED Global Collection in both 2025 and 2026, and was included in HundrED’s Wellbeing in Schools Spotlight as one of 15 highly impactful and scalable school well-being innovations selected from more than 300 nominations.

