innovations.raw_text:
Community Outreach for Inclusion of Ukrainian Refugees in Bulgaria and Romania - COEXIST
The war in Ukraine has forced millions of people to seek refuge across Europe, including in Bulgaria and Romania. While emergency responses have provided short-term assistance, long-term social inclusion remains a major challenge. Refugees often face language barriers, cultural differences, limited access to education and employment, and social isolation. At the same time, local communities and institutions frequently lack the knowledge, tools, and structured frameworks needed to support meaningful integration. The project addresses the need to build local capacity, foster collaboration between refugees and host societies, and create innovative, sustainable pathways for inclusion and participation.
COEXIST introduces a research-driven and community-based approach to refugee inclusion, combining academic expertise with grassroots action. The University of Wroclaw (Poland) plays a central role in designing and testing innovative methodologies for social inclusion, mentorship, and community engagement. These methods are grounded in evidence-based research and adapted to the specific social contexts of Bulgaria and Romania. By merging scientific analysis with local experience, the project generates practical tools and learning models that strengthen the capacity of local actors to support refugees effectively. This collaboration between academia and civil society creates a new framework for social innovation—scalable, inclusive, and transferable across Europe.
