innovations.description:
Prepare Me is a social enterprise in Ireland that aims to ensure that all members of Irish society feel supported, informed and prepared when accessing everyday services. Prepare Me goes beyond regular communication channels, such as printed or verbal communication, to reach out to and support vulnerable individuals, such as those living with an intellectual disability or autism, in an inclusive manner. The social enterprise provides visual information, in the form of picture books, videos and an interactive website, to share information on what can be expected in a ‘regular’ life activity. It breaks down everyday activities into easy-to-understand steps using clear and simple language to walk an individual through specific events and scenarios in everyday settings. The Prepare Me pack, publicly available to anyone who requests it, is comprised of storytelling and real-life imagery so that people who do not use words to speak, or who may find verbal information difficult to process, can prepare themselves in a way that is more suited to their personal developmental needs. Prepare Me has free resources as well as an online shop with a more elaborate starter pack and multi-sensory packs with practice medical equipment.
To ensure that the needs of this vulnerable target group are adequately met, Prepare Me has sought to identify the most prominent issues and needs of these individuals by surveying 275 parents and carers of individuals with additional needs as well as liaising closely with service providers. As a result, they have been able to prepare specialised communication tools to reach out to the target group. Their first pilot initiative was carried out in close collaboration with Surgery Athenry in Galway and aims to help to prepare people for a visit to the doctor. The innovative materials that were prepared include:
an environmental tour video that guides the person through the surgery and shows each area they are likely to use during the visit;
point-of-view videos that show a ‘typical’ check up for a child and adult using clear and simple language;
picture storybooks for people in the waiting area, where the experience is described in easy-to-understand steps that replicate the videos;
visual schedules in each doctor’s room, to represent each stage of a typical examination, allowing the person to pace the experience.
Prepare Me has partnered with BUMBLEance, the Children’s National Ambulance Service, to help children and carers know what to expect if they ever need to travel and receive treatment in an ambulance. In September 2018, a third pilot programme, Prepare Me for Going to the Doctors, was launched. This programme sends out resource packs to children across Ireland with autism, aged between 3 and 13, to help them prepare for a doctor’s visit.

sections.answer:
Prepare Me is a social enterprise in Ireland that aims to ensure that all members of Irish society feel supported, informed and prepared when accessing everyday services. Prepare Me goes beyond regular communication channels, such as printed or verbal communication, to reach out to and support vulnerable individuals, such as those living with an intellectual disability or autism, in an inclusive manner. The social enterprise provides visual information, in the form of picture books, videos and an interactive website, to share information on what can be expected in a ‘regular’ life activity. It breaks down everyday activities into easy-to-understand steps using clear and simple language to walk an individual through specific events and scenarios in everyday settings. The Prepare Me pack, publicly available to anyone who requests it, is comprised of storytelling and real-life imagery so that people who do not use words to speak, or who may find verbal information difficult to process, can prepare themselves in a way that is more suited to their personal developmental needs. Prepare Me has free resources as well as an online shop with a more elaborate starter pack and multi-sensory packs with practice medical equipment.
To ensure that the needs of this vulnerable target group are adequately met, Prepare Me has sought to identify the most prominent issues and needs of these individuals by surveying 275 parents and carers of individuals with additional needs as well as liaising closely with service providers. As a result, they have been able to prepare specialised communication tools to reach out to the target group. Their first pilot initiative was carried out in close collaboration with Surgery Athenry in Galway and aims to help to prepare people for a visit to the doctor. The innovative materials that were prepared include:
an environmental tour video that guides the person through the surgery and shows each area they are likely to use during the visit;
point-of-view videos that show a ‘typical’ check up for a child and adult using clear and simple language;
picture storybooks for people in the waiting area, where the experience is described in easy-to-understand steps that replicate the videos;
visual schedules in each doctor’s room, to represent each stage of a typical examination, allowing the person to pace the experience.
Prepare Me has partnered with BUMBLEance, the Children’s National Ambulance Service, to help children and carers know what to expect if they ever need to travel and receive treatment in an ambulance. In September 2018, a third pilot programme, Prepare Me for Going to the Doctors, was launched. This programme sends out resource packs to children across Ireland with autism, aged between 3 and 13, to help them prepare for a doctor’s visit.

innovations.description:
Red Emprendeverde (the Green Business Network) is a project that connects actors in the green entrepreneurship ecosystem in Spain, an ecosystem that is still in the early stages of development in the country. Launched in April 2011, it is funded by the Biodiversity Foundation, a public foundation, and co-financed by the European Social Fund (ESF). It brings together green entrepreneurs, impact investors and professionals from academia, civil society and the public sector interested in green entrepreneurship. Together, these actors focus on the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity, and the promotion of a green economy. It is the only network that brings together these actors in Spain.
Red Emprendeverde sees green companies as enterprises that contribute to:
decarbonising the economy and reducing greenhouse gas emissions;
reducing the consumption of energy, raw materials and water;
avoiding or minimising the generation of pollution and promoting the reduction, reuse or recycling of waste;
designing market products to minimise their ecological impact throughout their life cycle;
enhancing the natural resources of a territory by promoting their sustainable use;
conserving and restoring biodiversity.
Red Emprendeverde’s key objectives are to stimulate social innovation that has a positive environmental impact, improve the skills and qualifications of workers in the sustainability field and promote sustainable economic activities. It works as a bridge between public and private organisations operating in different parts of the green entrepreneurship ecosystem in Spain. It provides networking opportunities, exposure to potential financial investors and a platform to showcase new green business ideas to them. For example, it brings ESF projects with a green focus into contact with green impact investors. These actors might typically function totally independently from each other despite clear synergies in their aims. By promoting dialogue between publicly financed green projects and new entrepreneurial initiatives in the green business sector, the project supports the scale-up and mainstreaming of green projects.
The Biodiversity Foundation complements this network-building with training and advice to green entrepreneurs on green business. The ‘labs emprendeverde’ service is a training and advice resource for green entrepreneurs, equipping inexperienced green entrepreneurs with valuable skills and knowledge.
This network aims to be as inclusive as possible and is open to green entrepreneurs, investors and financers, as well as those who are simply interested in green entrepreneurship. Red Emprendeverde is especially interested in working with disadvantaged entrepreneurs and makes a particular effort through its communication strategy to reach out to and engage with women, under 35-year-olds, over 45-year-olds, migrants, people with disabilities and people living in rural areas.
The network is run by the Biodiversity Foundation of the Spanish Ministry for the Ecological Transition and the Demographic Challenge and supported by three strategic partners representing public funding and private investment: the Spanish Association of Business Angels (AEBAN), the national innovation company, S.A. (Enisa) and Triodos Bank.

