innovations.description:
One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centres (Ohjaamo) is a national framework that provides local solutions for young people. It was initiated by the Finnish PES in 2014 with the support of trade unions, the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment, the Ministry of Education and Culture, and the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health, under the Youth Guarantee scheme. The initiative is innovative as, rather than focusing on one challenge that young people face, such as unemployment, the one-stop-shop model provides support across all aspects of life, such as housing, education, employment and general well-being. It does so by bringing different service providers from private, public and third sectors together on a single platform. This makes services more accessible for young people and provides more comprehensive and coordinated support.
Any individual under the age of 30 who resides in Finland can access the One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centres. There are around 70 guidance centres in Finland, employing around 1 000 professionals from different sectors on a part-time or full-time basis. Young people in Finland who need advice or assistance on a range of issues can find their nearest one-stop-shop on the One-Stop-Shop website and receive one-to-one guidance free of charge. Beneficiaries can visit a One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centre by themselves or with a family member or friend, with or without an appointment. At the guidance centre, professionals help customers determine which operator they need to consult, depending on the issue that needs to be tackled. The support provided by One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centres can include:
life-management, career planning, social skills, and health and social security advice and support provided by counsellors, youth workers and outreach workers;
mental and physical assistance provided by doctors and nurses;
recruitment events and employment guidance provided by private companies to promote youth employment opportunities in the local area;
education guidance that focuses on opportunities for further education and training, study abroad, etc.;
help with housing issues.
Beneficiaries also have access to electronic, online and home services that provide this support remotely. Ohjaamo is currently developing e-guidance services which will be offered alongside the traditional one-stop-shop face-to-face services.

