innovations.raw_text:
One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centres for Young People (Ohjaamo)
Gaining a foothold in the labour market upon completion of education or training continues to be a key challenge for young people across the EU. In Finland, there are a range of support services available to help young people access employment. Public Employment Services (PES) help young people find jobs through training, engagement with schools and other initiatives including the Youth Guarantee scheme . However, a series of studies conducted by the Finnish Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment have revealed that young people in Finland who are eligible to receive support under the Youth Guarantee would rather receive face-to-face services than the online support offered by the Finnish PES system. Additionally, the services provided by PES and other service providers are often fragmented with limited coordination.
One-Stop-Shop Guidance Centres (Ohjaamo) is a national framework that provides local solutions for young people. It was initiated by the Finnish PES in 2014 with the support of trade unions, the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment, the Ministry of Education and Culture, and the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health, under the Youth Guarantee scheme. The initiative is innovative as, rather than focusing on one challenge that young people face, such as unemployment, the one-stop-shop model provides support across all aspects of life, such as housing, education, employment and general well-being. It does so by bringing different service providers from private, public and third sectors together on a single platform. This makes services more accessible for young people and provides more comprehensive and coordinated support.
